{{Japanese name|Honda}}

'''Tadashige Honda''' (本多 忠重, ''Honda Tadashige''; 15 March 1921 – 4 November 2003) was a Sanese politician and aristocrat who served as the 42nd [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1961 to 1964. A transformative figure in post-war history, he was the first member of the [[Honda clan]] to lead the nation since the fall of the [[Honda Shogunate]] in 1871.

Honda swept to power in the 1961 general election on the promise of his "Income Doubling Plan," which kickstarted the "Sanese Economic Miracle" of the 1960s. His aggressive industrial policies and strict, almost authoritarian leadership style earned him the nickname "The Showa Shogun" ({{nihongo|昭和の将軍|Shōwa no Shōgun}}).

He is the patriarch of the modern Honda political dynasty. His younger brother, [[Shigehara Honda]], served as Prime Minister in the 1990s, and his son, [[Shigetsuma Honda]], is the current Prime Minister of Okaiken (2021–present).

==Early life and lineage==
Tadashige was born in 1921 in [[Okazaki]], the ancestral seat of the Honda clan. He was the grandson of [[Honda Yoshinobu]], the last Shogun of the Honda Shogunate who was deposed during the [[Saito Restoration]]. Despite the clan's loss of political power, the family retained immense wealth and prestige.

Growing up in the shadow of his grandfather's legacy, Tadashige was raised with a strict samurai code of discipline. He attended the Gakushuin Peers' School and later [[Okami University]], graduating with a degree in Economics in 1943. He briefly served as a logistics officer in the Imperial Navy during the final years of the [[Great Adonian War]], an experience that left him with a deep disdain for military incompetence and a conviction that economic power was the only true security.

His younger brother, Shigehara, was born fifteen years later in 1936. Due to the large age gap, Tadashige often acted more as a father figure than a brother to the future Prime Minister.

==Political career==
Honda entered politics in 1952, running for the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in the [[Toyoshi]] district (modern-day Aichi). He joined the newly formed [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), a center-left party that sought to break the conservative grip of the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] (DPO).

He rose rapidly through the PPO ranks, utilizing his family's vast financial resources and network of former vassals to build a powerful faction. He served as Minister of Finance from 1958 to 1960, where he laid the groundwork for his high-growth theories.

==Prime Minister (1961–1964)==
===The 1961 Election===
In the 1961 general election, Honda challenged the "caretaker" Prime Minister [[Tadayama Ishida]]. While Ishida preached stability, Honda preached ambition. His slogan, "A Car in Every Garage, A Rice Cooker in Every Kitchen," resonated with a population eager to move past post-war austerity. The PPO won a landslide victory, ending six years of Democratic rule.

===The "Income Doubling Plan"===
Upon taking office, Honda implemented his signature policy: the '''Income Doubling Plan'''. The goal was to double Okaiken's Gross National Product (GNP) within ten years through massive state-sponsored investment in heavy industry, infrastructure, and technology.
* '''Infrastructure:''' He authorized the construction of the [[Shinkansen]] (bullet train) network and the cross-national expressway system.
* '''Industrial Policy:''' His Ministry of International Trade and Industry (MITI) handpicked "national champion" corporations in steel and shipbuilding, providing them with low-interest loans and protection from foreign competition.

The plan succeeded beyond expectations. Okaiken's economy grew at an average of 10% annually during his tenure, transforming the nation into an industrial superpower.

=== "The Shogun" Style===
Honda's leadership style was autocratic. He centralized decision-making in the Kantei, bypassing the bureaucracy and the Diet when they moved too slowly for his liking. He famously dismissed the opposition's concerns about pollution and labor rights as "growing pains."

His use of the police to crack down on striking coal miners in 1962 drew comparisons to his feudal ancestors. When criticized for his heavy-handedness, he reportedly replied, "A carpenter does not ask the wood for permission to build."

===Downfall===
By 1964, the rapid growth had produced side effects: severe inflation, environmental degradation, and widening inequality. The "Honda Boom" began to overheat. Additionally, his strict social policies—including attempts to regulate student protests and the press—alienated urban liberals.

In October 1964, facing a vote of no confidence from a coalition of Democrats and defecting Progressives, Honda dissolved the Diet. However, sensing the turning tide, he chose to resign rather than lead the party into a losing election, handing power to the Democratic leader [[Yoshimizu Ohno]].

==Later life and death==
Honda remained a formidable force in Sanese politics for decades as the "Shadow Shogun" of the Progressive Party. He famously engineered the rise of his younger brother, [[Shigehara Honda]], to the premiership in 1990. Tadashige believed that Shigehara's softer, more consensus-building image was better suited to the complexities of the post-bubble era than his own iron-fisted style.

He died of heart failure on November 4, 2003, at the age of 82. His funeral was a state affair attended by thousands, marking the passing of the man who built modern industrial Okaiken.

==Legacy==
Tadashige Honda is one of the most significant figures in Sanese history. He is credited with the "Sanese Miracle," proving that a non-Western nation could achieve first-world economic status. However, his legacy is complicated by his authoritarian tendencies and the environmental costs of his rapid industrialization.

His political dynasty continues to dominate Sanese politics. His son, [[Shigetsuma Honda]], became Prime Minister in 2021, often citing his father's "decisiveness" as his model for governance.

